I desperately want to believe studying could finally pay off, but after commuting over thirty minutes past crumbling roads in Pietermaritzburg just to sit in classrooms where our teachers have stopped trying, I refuse to hand over another subscription when my grandmother’s R10 000 monthly income already gets swallowed by our R4 001–R8 000 school fees. R50 a month sounds manageable until it forces me to choose between buying data for past papers or putting extra groceries on our table, so I simply cannot justify a recurring cost when financial rewards feel like just another empty promise. If Thuto actually paid out verified cash after my first set of exams and let me freeze payments when our household budget runs tight, I would reconsider, but until then I’m keeping every rand where it’s guaranteed to survive.
