R50 a month. That is money that leaves the house. You talk about turning study into cash, but I am paying upfront to see if the system pays me back later. The grant and my gogo’s pension sit at ten thousand rand. School fees take their cut. After that, we count what is left. I am not saying the idea does not sound sharp. But sharp does not buy data. It does not switch the power back on when stage four hits, and it does not bring water back to the street taps. You want fifty rand every month. That is a bill. Bills do not wait. If the taxi fare jumps, or the load-shedding drags on, that fifty rand goes straight to the kitchen. I can want a thing and still know the numbers do not bend. You are asking for a recurring cost in a house that already stretches. If it was free, or if it replaced the extra maths notes I am already buying, I would sit and look at it. But paying to study? The economy is shrinking. We are just trying to hold the line.

