Fifty rand a month is not a lot on paper, but it is another bill that comes around every thirty days. My household brings in fifteen thousand rand a month between my mother’s municipal clerk salary and the child support grant, but that money has to cover food, transport, and the school fees. When you look at it that way, fifty rand a month competes with real essentials. I walk twenty minutes to school each morning, keeping my head down because break-ins and trouble are just part of the streets now, and I only use my phone for internet when I can manage the data. I see the municipality trying to fix the broken streetlights and sort out the water cuts, and I trust the system will eventually sort things out, but I also know that complaints usually go nowhere and services are not keeping up. Between load-shedding and everything else, keeping up is hard enough without adding another charge. The idea of getting financial rewards for studying sounds useful when the job market is shrinking every year, but I already study because I need to pass, not for a payout. A system that pays you to learn might just make me work for the reward instead of learning for myself. I want to see how it works, but I cannot just add a monthly charge without knowing if my household can actually carry it. Wanting something and affording it are not the same thing.

