Fifty rand a month is a number we have to look at carefully. My mother brings home fifteen thousand rand a month for our house, and the child support grant helps us keep the pantry stocked. School fees already take between one thousand and two thousand rand a year, which is a fixed cost we have to plan around before the term even starts. The idea of turning study time into actual rewards sounds useful, and I trust that a proper platform can deliver on what it promises. But a monthly subscription means it keeps coming back, and when you walk through Mthatha each day past broken streetlights and keep a careful eye on the streets, you learn to watch exactly where the money goes. Load-shedding and water cuts remind us that services are not keeping up, even though the municipality is trying to sort things out. I would want something that boosts motivation, but I cannot justify taking fifty rand from the household every month when it competes with food, transport, and the school fees we already pay. There is some room in our budget, but it gets eaten up by the things that cannot wait. The economy is getting tighter, and I know jobs are shrinking after matric, so every rand has to be placed where it is truly needed. If the school could absorb the cost like they handle our lower band fees, or if I could use it without touching our monthly cash first, then I would give it proper consideration.

