I hear what you are saying. Turning study effort into money sounds like it could help, but I have to look at what it means for my house. My mother brings in fifteen thousand rand a month with her municipal clerk job and the child support grant, and that covers food, transport, and the school fees that sit in the R1 001 to R2 000 band for the whole year. There is not much room after that. Fifty rand a month is a new cost that has to come from somewhere that already needs to pay for things we cannot skip. I might want to try it because the idea of getting something back for studying makes sense to me, but wanting it and being able to pay for it are two different things. We only have internet on our phones, not a computer, so using it would also mean spending data on top of the subscription. I walk past break-ins and trouble every day on my way to class, and I know jobs are shrinking, so we have to be careful with what leaves our account. I still trust the school and the education department to handle things properly, so if the platform really works, maybe they could cover the fee for learners who need it instead of putting it on families. I am not saying the idea is bad. I am just saying the monthly payment does not fit what we can afford right now.

