Fifty rand a month sounds small on paper, but when a household is working with ten thousand rand a month, any recurring charge sits directly beside the groceries and transport. I process compliance documents at the municipality every day, so I know how to track what goes out and what stays in. The concept of turning study effort into financial rewards makes sense to me. It fits with how I see things: things are improving, and if the children put in the work, they should see a return. That is the kind of forward push we try to keep in our home. But wanting a tool that motivates them is entirely separate from making room for it in the budget. I watch the school fee deadlines and the load-shedding schedules closely because they dictate our month. A fifty rand subscription is not a one-off purchase. It is a monthly commitment that has to compete with real essentials. I would like to see it work, but I cannot simply slot it into our accounts until I know exactly what value it delivers against the fixed costs we already carry. If I can see a clear return in their results within the first term, or if there is a way to trial it without locking us into the monthly cycle, then we can sit down and look at whether the numbers actually balance.

