Fifty rand a month sounds modest on paper, but when you’re stretching ten thousand rand across groceries, transport, and school fees for four children, every recurring line item has to prove it belongs. I’ve always treated education as the most reliable ladder out of tight circumstances, so I’m willing to listen if something genuinely sharpens focus and lifts results. But the moment I hear that study effort gets converted into cash payouts, my guard goes up. After long shifts at the municipality and the evening homework sessions that leave my eyes heavy, what I’m trying to build in my children isn’t a transactional habit—it’s the quiet discipline to sit down, work through the material, and keep going because they understand the long game. If this platform conditions them to expect money every time they open a textbook, I fear we’re trading intrinsic drive for a short-term trick. That hesitation sits right alongside the practical reality of one shared smartphone, the data bundles I already have to ration, and the simple fact that I can’t afford to add another monthly drain unless it clearly builds independent study skills, runs reliably without constant connectivity, and shows it improves marks without relying on external carrots. I appreciate the intention behind it, but until I can see it teaching self-motivation rather than just dangling rewards, I can’t justify moving it into our budget.

