The idea of getting actual money for studying sounds like a fair exchange. It is right to want young people to see a return for their effort, especially when the household depends on the child support grant and piece jobs that do not always come through on time. We have some headroom in the monthly five thousand rand, but a recurring cost competes directly with real essentials. That fifty rand comes out of the same pool we use for food and the shared mobile connection. When the gravel road washes out after heavy rain, or when the load-shedding schedule cuts the power, we are already managing with less. I want to use it if it helps me get ahead, but I cannot ignore the monthly drain on a budget that is already stretched. The teachers at our school do their best with what they have, and I believe the municipality means to deliver services even when the taps run dry, but a subscription fee is a fixed cost that does not wait for our situation to improve. I also wonder if I will have the quiet space and the airtime to use it properly when the phone is shared with my aunt and her work contacts. If the platform charged only when we had clear headroom, or if the cost sat below what we already spend on data, it would be easier to justify. Right now, I am watching the numbers, and fifty rand every month feels like a step we are not ready to take.

