The idea of getting something back for putting hours into your books sounds right to me. If you work, it makes sense to see a return. But I have to look at the numbers before I say I can join. Our household brings in R5000 a month, and that has to stretch across the child support grant, my aunt’s piece jobs, food, and the daily basics. R50 every month is a standing charge that does not go away. It sits higher than the school fees band of R301 to R500 for the entire year. We already plan our spending carefully, and a monthly subscription pulls from money that needs to stay available for airtime and data on our shared mobile connection. I want to use a platform that rewards study, and I do not doubt the effort put into building it. I just cannot justify a recurring payment when we have to plan our reading around the load-shedding schedule and the gravel road to town cuts off after rain. I know the local council means well, but the taps run dry and complaints go nowhere, and the economy is getting worse for families in our position. I still believe my teachers and the government are trying their best, but for now I have to keep my spending where the household needs it most. If there was a once-off payment or a way to access the study tools without a standing monthly charge, I would reconsider. Until then, it is just not right to commit.

