Fifty rand a month just to access a platform that says it will pay you for studying? That does not sound right to me. At home we count what we have very carefully, and my aunt’s piece work plus the child support grant only stretch so far. We manage to cover the basics and even keep a little headroom, but a recurring fifty rand pull still competes directly with airtime and the essentials we need to keep moving. I know things are getting harder for families like mine, and I can see how services around us are struggling, but that just means we have to be sharper about where our money goes. I want to do well because I know it is the only way to get to university and build a proper future, not because I am waiting for a cash payout to make me open my books. If the tool was free, or if it actually cost less than the data bundles I already buy to look up notes, I would sit with it properly. But right now, paying a subscription to earn money is a circle that does not close when there is no real slack in the household budget.

